1
00:00:02,639 --> 00:00:09,124
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is a sutta in the Anguttara Nikaya Book of Sevens, 7.49.

2
00:00:12,006 --> 00:00:16,770
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in regard to seven perceptions that are encouraged to be developed.

3
00:00:20,013 --> 00:00:26,278
[SPEAKER_00]: The first one is the perception of a subha, or the non-beautiful.

4
00:00:28,179 --> 00:00:29,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's call it the ugly.

5
00:00:31,678 --> 00:00:40,343
[SPEAKER_00]: When the perception of the ugly is developed and frequently practiced, it is of great fruit and great benefit.

6
00:00:41,324 --> 00:00:55,613
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when a bhikkhu often lives with such a purpose, his mind shrinks back and goes in the opposite direction and turns away from engaging in sexual intercourse.

7
00:00:55,653 --> 00:00:58,615
[SPEAKER_00]: So, either equanimity

8
00:01:00,135 --> 00:01:09,840
[SPEAKER_00]: All the opposite direction of what the mind is attending to, gets established, if he practices this asubha.

9
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:21,665
[SPEAKER_00]: So, either his mind turns away from sexual intercourse, or he gets based in equanimity, or his mind gets established in that opposite direction.

10
00:01:22,506 --> 00:01:22,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Of what?

11
00:01:22,946 --> 00:01:25,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Of what he's attending to, which is the beautiful.

12
00:01:25,767 --> 00:01:25,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

13
00:01:26,128 --> 00:01:26,308
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

14
00:01:27,108 --> 00:01:32,089
[SPEAKER_00]: And that Pāḷi term is patikula manasikāra, usually translated as repulsive.

15
00:01:32,169 --> 00:01:34,610
[SPEAKER_00]: So, his mind gets established in the repulsive.

16
00:01:35,090 --> 00:01:37,271
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's, it's the other...

17
00:01:37,291 --> 00:01:38,051
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the other side.

18
00:01:38,151 --> 00:01:40,792
[SPEAKER_00]: The other side of what he's attending to.

19
00:01:41,512 --> 00:01:43,953
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's the other side of what is being attended.

20
00:01:44,013 --> 00:01:45,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Pāṭi, against.

21
00:01:46,893 --> 00:01:56,276
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's against the directly opposite, as in the opposite side of the beautiful.

22
00:01:57,521 --> 00:02:04,023
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the side that's simultaneously there, not the opposite side in response to beauty.

23
00:02:04,243 --> 00:02:06,503
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the point, the opposite side.

24
00:02:08,984 --> 00:02:09,644
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you know?

25
00:02:10,785 --> 00:02:16,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, in order to define an opposite, you need to have that thing that this opposes it.

26
00:02:17,787 --> 00:02:22,848
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the whole point of the opposite side is to recognize that the ugly

27
00:02:24,263 --> 00:02:29,387
[SPEAKER_01]: is present opposite to the beautiful, while beautiful is there.

28
00:02:31,149 --> 00:02:41,758
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not something you need to find, like, in response to beautiful, but it's more like something you need to discern within the beautiful as the opposite side.

29
00:02:42,611 --> 00:02:48,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, if you say the two sides of the coin, you don't seek the second coin to see the other side of it.

30
00:02:49,213 --> 00:02:53,955
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same coin, but now you need to see the opposite side, the one you're not looking at.

31
00:02:54,335 --> 00:03:06,440
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why that's so important is because most people, most commonly, the way people practice the asubha is in response to the beautiful, as in you want to get rid of the beautiful and replace it with the ugly.

32
00:03:07,397 --> 00:03:09,178
[SPEAKER_01]: So, those are just two separate things for you.

33
00:03:09,858 --> 00:03:16,982
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not seeing that ugly within that beautiful, which is how you would cancel the beautiful, by seeing the opposite of it within it.

34
00:03:18,563 --> 00:03:29,668
[SPEAKER_01]: But instead, you're just trying to get rid of the beautiful, because it bothers you, and replace it with ugly, because that supposedly not bothers you, or it shouldn't bother you as much, if you want to practice the restraint.

35
00:03:30,128 --> 00:03:33,670
[SPEAKER_01]: But in reality, what you're doing is just acting on account of how you're feeling.

36
00:03:35,126 --> 00:03:40,010
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the beautiful is there, bothering you, and you just want to be free from bother, which is fine.

37
00:03:40,290 --> 00:03:42,312
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be free from bother, but how you go about it?

38
00:03:42,352 --> 00:03:44,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you go about blaming the beautiful for the bother?

39
00:03:45,875 --> 00:03:51,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, implicitly, yes, because you're trying to get rid of the beautiful by replacing it with the ugly, which you're supposed to be cultivating.

40
00:03:54,122 --> 00:03:57,184
[SPEAKER_01]: But that means you're just... you don't really know where the bother is.

41
00:03:57,464 --> 00:03:58,965
[SPEAKER_01]: And the bother is not in the beautiful.

42
00:03:59,906 --> 00:04:01,147
[SPEAKER_01]: The lust is not in the beautiful.

43
00:04:01,187 --> 00:04:02,268
[SPEAKER_01]: None of that is in the beautiful.

44
00:04:03,629 --> 00:04:04,430
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in your own...

45
00:04:07,404 --> 00:04:11,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Lack of understanding of it, not seeing the nature of it, attraction to it, and so on.

46
00:04:12,747 --> 00:04:17,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because you don't see the nature of that beautiful thing, that's why you're attracted to it.

47
00:04:17,250 --> 00:04:18,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

48
00:04:18,591 --> 00:04:27,776
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and you will not see the nature, because now you think, oh, the attraction, the whole thing is in the beautiful, so all I need to do is replace the beautiful with these images of ugly, and then I'm free from attraction.

49
00:04:27,836 --> 00:04:27,976
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

50
00:04:28,656 --> 00:04:31,277
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what happened to many monks who were practicing wrongly.

51
00:04:31,297 --> 00:04:33,339
[SPEAKER_01]: Then they started getting attracted to the ugly.

52
00:04:33,779 --> 00:04:33,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

53
00:04:34,655 --> 00:04:37,356
[SPEAKER_01]: So, while beautiful is present, you have a chance

54
00:04:51,823 --> 00:05:05,059
[SPEAKER_01]: To discern correctly, that like yoniso, manasikāra, to attend correctly, to attend to the opposite side of it, that is there, implicit, which is why freedom from the side that is beautiful, is possible.

55
00:05:07,891 --> 00:05:12,775
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't have to do anything in response to it, you just need to see through it.

56
00:05:13,396 --> 00:05:23,944
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, if you actually develop that, doesn't matter what type of beautiful perception you encounter later on in the future, the mind remains equally developed in regard to it.

57
00:05:24,024 --> 00:05:27,407
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind sees through it, because it has seen through its nature.

58
00:05:28,914 --> 00:05:36,198
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that beautiful is based on that which is not beautiful.

59
00:05:36,838 --> 00:05:38,359
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's inseparable from.

60
00:05:38,499 --> 00:05:39,039
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the thing.

61
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:43,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you will not necessarily see there's based upon it.

62
00:05:44,844 --> 00:05:50,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you start with the beautiful being put first, the nature of ignorance.

63
00:05:50,267 --> 00:05:54,770
[SPEAKER_01]: It shouldn't be first, but being put first, there's no first point.

64
00:05:55,311 --> 00:06:00,774
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no... it's beginningless, the attitude that putting the beautiful first, assuming the beautiful.

65
00:06:01,675 --> 00:06:06,218
[SPEAKER_01]: It shouldn't be there, because if you look through it, you just see the ugly, but it is there.

66
00:06:06,238 --> 00:06:11,361
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you start as a person with a mind that's not free from ignorance.

67
00:06:11,381 --> 00:06:14,543
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why if you uproot it, you cannot go back to it.

68
00:06:15,520 --> 00:06:24,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, actually, if you see through the nature of beautiful, as inseparable from ugly, then you realize this is not beautiful, it's just less ugly.

69
00:06:25,146 --> 00:06:26,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Not as ugly, but equally ugly.

70
00:06:27,747 --> 00:06:30,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means equally start serving as a basis for dispassion.

71
00:06:33,531 --> 00:06:38,093
[SPEAKER_01]: And that brings me to, I want to say, when you read that first, the opening, like, oh, he cultivates perception.

72
00:06:38,133 --> 00:06:40,695
[SPEAKER_01]: How, like, how do you cultivate the perception?

73
00:06:41,665 --> 00:06:41,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

74
00:06:41,965 --> 00:06:42,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Attending to...

75
00:06:42,546 --> 00:06:43,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Attending to certain perceptions.

76
00:06:43,966 --> 00:06:44,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

77
00:06:44,287 --> 00:06:47,168
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can either attend to the beautiful or you can...

78
00:07:11,554 --> 00:07:11,954
[SPEAKER_01]: What else?

79
00:07:12,975 --> 00:07:13,435
[SPEAKER_00]: By knowing.

80
00:07:13,715 --> 00:07:18,717
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to... say, you want to establish your mind in the perception of ugly, now.

81
00:07:19,937 --> 00:07:21,198
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you... how would you do that?

82
00:07:32,822 --> 00:07:34,523
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you discern the opposite side?

83
00:07:36,604 --> 00:07:38,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, look at the the context.

84
00:07:39,785 --> 00:07:40,706
[SPEAKER_00]: See the bigger picture.

85
00:07:40,846 --> 00:07:41,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Of what?

86
00:07:41,706 --> 00:07:44,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Of that beautiful... whatever I'm finding beautiful.

87
00:07:44,547 --> 00:07:44,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

88
00:07:44,827 --> 00:07:46,148
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need the beautiful first.

89
00:07:47,268 --> 00:07:51,530
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you... and that's... that's when the... when we would say you should, like, keep

90
00:07:54,298 --> 00:07:58,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Rechecking, so to speak, your mind.

91
00:07:58,280 --> 00:07:59,921
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there lost in it now?

92
00:08:00,281 --> 00:08:05,343
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, is there presently enduring attraction to beautiful right here, right now?

93
00:08:06,064 --> 00:08:07,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Then the answer is no.

94
00:08:07,184 --> 00:08:12,287
[SPEAKER_01]: But then it's like, oh, but could there be lost in this mind?

95
00:08:13,247 --> 00:08:21,131
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, in the future, in the moments to come, is my mind liable?

96
00:08:22,305 --> 00:08:25,187
[SPEAKER_01]: To become attracted again to something of sensual nature.

97
00:08:26,028 --> 00:08:26,428
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's it.

98
00:08:26,448 --> 00:08:34,313
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not just about, oh, now I have to go and look, or taste, or touch, or engage in any form with sensual objects in order to see the ugly through it.

99
00:08:34,353 --> 00:08:34,494
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

100
00:08:34,574 --> 00:08:35,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Quite the opposite.

101
00:08:36,415 --> 00:08:46,362
[SPEAKER_01]: By not engaging through sensual objects, practicing sense exchange, you get to see the liability towards the attraction, which is present attraction.

102
00:08:47,395 --> 00:08:55,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you are not... you will not be liable to be attracted to things in the future, if that liability is not presently enduring as a form of attraction already.

103
00:08:57,502 --> 00:09:05,787
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's when people sometimes judge how you... you need to... almost like how you need to flirt with your kilesas, to see... to learn about them.

104
00:09:05,827 --> 00:09:05,987
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

105
00:09:06,187 --> 00:09:06,848
[SPEAKER_01]: Quite the opposite.

106
00:09:07,428 --> 00:09:09,789
[SPEAKER_01]: You're actually flirting with... giving in to the weakness.

107
00:09:09,829 --> 00:09:13,632
[SPEAKER_01]: By giving in to the weakness, you're not going to see your liability, because you're already acting out of it.

108
00:09:16,200 --> 00:09:28,986
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to not engage with sensuality, and then see that another reflection that will say, so, is my mind liable to future arising of these things?

109
00:09:29,006 --> 00:09:32,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Can lust, greed, aversion, delusion, arise in this mind?

110
00:09:32,508 --> 00:09:34,969
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not here now, but could they be here?

111
00:09:35,549 --> 00:09:37,030
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says, yes, they can be here.

112
00:09:37,490 --> 00:09:38,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, why will this thing?

113
00:09:38,851 --> 00:09:43,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because I haven't abandoned things right here, right now, on account of which lust arises.

114
00:09:45,282 --> 00:09:50,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is craving.

115
00:09:51,307 --> 00:09:53,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Depending which angle you want to take on it.

116
00:09:54,270 --> 00:10:05,079
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is, you want to recognize that the possibility of last aversion delusion manifesting in your experience is already last aversion delusion in your experience, just on a subtler level.

117
00:10:05,640 --> 00:10:10,264
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to wait for the central last aversion delusion to say, okay, now I have it.

118
00:10:11,357 --> 00:10:15,061
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to restrain from it and then recognize the possibility of those things arising in the future.

119
00:10:15,721 --> 00:10:25,070
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, how would you know this possibility of lust arising in the future, unless that possibility of lust is already present, right here, right now, which is how you know it.

120
00:10:28,226 --> 00:10:31,488
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you can discern that possibility...

121
00:10:31,508 --> 00:10:34,369
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's... that's already... that's basically the roots of lust.

122
00:10:34,990 --> 00:10:41,693
[SPEAKER_01]: See, the possibility of lust, as the Buddha said, is because... is there, because basis for lust is there.

123
00:10:41,813 --> 00:10:42,694
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't abandoned it.

124
00:10:43,154 --> 00:10:52,179
[SPEAKER_01]: So, circumstantially, you're not particularly, specifically experiencing any lust right here, right now, but the whole basis, the whole domain of being liable to lust is still there.

125
00:10:52,299 --> 00:10:53,460
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't abandoned it.

126
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why, if you look at it, can these things arise in the future?

127
00:10:56,922 --> 00:10:59,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, means, oh, I'm presently still maintaining their basis.

128
00:11:00,465 --> 00:11:05,549
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the conditions are right, lust will arise on account of the basis that I'm maintaining.

129
00:11:06,069 --> 00:11:07,010
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the whole point.

130
00:11:09,431 --> 00:11:10,872
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what you recognize.

131
00:11:10,933 --> 00:11:13,955
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what would be the basis for lust arising?

132
00:11:17,117 --> 00:11:18,198
[SPEAKER_00]: The future lust arising.

133
00:11:23,254 --> 00:11:23,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Valuing.

134
00:11:24,475 --> 00:11:24,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

135
00:11:24,996 --> 00:11:25,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Valuing of pleasure.

136
00:11:27,257 --> 00:11:28,078
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no pleasure.

137
00:11:28,278 --> 00:11:28,478
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

138
00:11:28,518 --> 00:11:30,039
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not engaging in an essential pleasure.

139
00:11:30,079 --> 00:11:34,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you're maintaining the view that that pleasure is valuable.

140
00:11:35,042 --> 00:11:36,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Or not even that.

141
00:11:37,024 --> 00:11:40,326
[SPEAKER_01]: It's enough to maintain the view that that pleasure is not dangerous.

142
00:11:41,767 --> 00:11:45,369
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's enough to maintain the basis for future lust to arise.

143
00:11:45,489 --> 00:11:46,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Not seeing danger.

144
00:11:46,931 --> 00:11:52,774
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly if you see the extent of the danger, as the Buddha would say, you free yourself from sensuality.

145
00:11:52,794 --> 00:11:54,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is the extent of gratification.

146
00:11:56,356 --> 00:12:03,960
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as I haven't understood the danger, the extent of the danger in regard to that gratification, I did not claim I am fully free from sensual desires.

147
00:12:04,721 --> 00:12:10,003
[SPEAKER_01]: But when I understood the danger, then you understood the escape, then he claimed that he's fully free from sensual desires.

148
00:12:12,665 --> 00:12:16,407
[SPEAKER_01]: So, within the beautiful, the opposite side of the beautiful is the danger.

149
00:12:17,320 --> 00:12:19,282
[SPEAKER_01]: It's defined by that beautiful, by the way.

150
00:12:20,403 --> 00:12:27,770
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a separate thing you have to take on as a separate contemplation, as a result of witnessing the beautiful.

151
00:12:27,830 --> 00:12:29,011
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's...

152
00:12:29,051 --> 00:12:34,476
[SPEAKER_01]: There is the beautiful, perception of beautiful, perception of attraction, perception of lust, enduring in this mind.

153
00:12:37,499 --> 00:12:40,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Within that, I need to discern that thing as dangerous.

154
00:12:43,023 --> 00:12:49,927
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could already see, like, cultivation of perception is not done on the particular level of this perception, that perception.

155
00:12:49,967 --> 00:12:51,827
[SPEAKER_01]: It's done on the level of the domain of perception.

156
00:12:53,008 --> 00:12:58,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you are perceiving the fact that you are liable to future lust, to future centrality.

157
00:12:59,311 --> 00:13:00,632
[SPEAKER_01]: That is a perception in your mind.

158
00:13:01,052 --> 00:13:03,633
[SPEAKER_01]: That liability is seen, is perceived.

159
00:13:04,914 --> 00:13:10,317
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not in regard to anything particular experienced by eyes, nose, mouth, ears, and so on.

160
00:13:11,455 --> 00:13:14,476
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the cultivation of perceptions.

161
00:13:14,496 --> 00:13:19,778
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he called, like, in other sutras, perception of light, perception of boundlessness.

162
00:13:20,518 --> 00:13:21,919
[SPEAKER_01]: None of these things are specific.

163
00:13:22,459 --> 00:13:24,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, can you see boundlessness with your eyes?

164
00:13:24,680 --> 00:13:24,840
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

165
00:13:25,220 --> 00:13:25,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

166
00:13:27,813 --> 00:13:35,135
[SPEAKER_01]: So, these perceptions are mental in regard to the sense domain, in regard to the eyes and so on, in regard to sense organs.

167
00:13:35,695 --> 00:13:36,495
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's another thing.

168
00:13:36,535 --> 00:13:39,196
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about looking at this, not looking at that.

169
00:13:39,696 --> 00:13:48,798
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about seeing the liability to pleasure, to lust, to craving on the level of your mind, perceiving there and seeing... perceiving the danger in it.

170
00:13:48,978 --> 00:13:50,378
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another perception you develop.

171
00:13:51,432 --> 00:13:54,353
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger within this perception is also perception.

172
00:13:55,294 --> 00:14:03,158
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you cultivate it long enough, next time your eye sees beautiful, the danger is seen through that beautiful.

173
00:14:03,598 --> 00:14:04,238
[SPEAKER_01]: With your eyes.

174
00:14:04,538 --> 00:14:06,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you develop it sufficiently with your mind.

175
00:14:07,880 --> 00:14:08,900
[SPEAKER_01]: The significance of it.

176
00:14:09,321 --> 00:14:12,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Before it's even... before the particular beautiful thing has arisen.

177
00:14:12,782 --> 00:14:13,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

178
00:14:13,503 --> 00:14:15,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Before... that has been developed before.

179
00:14:15,183 --> 00:14:18,005
[SPEAKER_01]: But what I'm saying is, if you're free from sensuality,

180
00:14:19,061 --> 00:14:27,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Even when a particular essential thing has arisen and seen, it's the danger is seen, not the beautiful of it, which requires ignoring the danger.

181
00:14:28,746 --> 00:14:29,287
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

182
00:14:29,487 --> 00:14:41,073
[SPEAKER_01]: You, through development of that right perception of danger, the opposite side implicit in it, seeing it, learning how to basically maintain it in its enduring,

183
00:14:42,957 --> 00:14:47,199
[SPEAKER_01]: That will culminate to the point of where you will stop putting that beautiful first.

184
00:14:47,759 --> 00:14:49,460
[SPEAKER_01]: That will stop being the reference point.

185
00:14:49,900 --> 00:14:52,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, it's the danger and the ugliness is the reference point.

186
00:14:53,082 --> 00:14:58,605
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then the difference is only like how ugly and how dangerous it is.

187
00:14:58,625 --> 00:15:01,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like it's not dangerous, it's beautiful and then sometimes gets... No.

188
00:15:01,866 --> 00:15:06,789
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always ugly, it's always dangerous, just sometimes it's more dangerous, sometimes it's less dangerous.

189
00:15:06,809 --> 00:15:08,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you don't go near it.

190
00:15:09,865 --> 00:15:12,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes I'm near the poison, sometimes poison is far away.

191
00:15:12,887 --> 00:15:15,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, I'm not engaging with it, because it's poison.

192
00:15:23,496 --> 00:15:31,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, so it says here that opposite side gets established once you have developed that perception.

193
00:15:33,203 --> 00:15:35,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are the things that your mind will turn away from.

194
00:15:36,246 --> 00:15:36,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

195
00:15:38,220 --> 00:15:39,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Prospects of sensuality.

196
00:15:41,322 --> 00:15:43,703
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be dispassionate, equanimous towards...

197
00:15:44,064 --> 00:15:48,947
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the development of perception, then, means development of the right characteristics.

198
00:15:49,707 --> 00:15:52,229
[SPEAKER_01]: They're implicit in it, but you choose to ignore them.

199
00:15:53,309 --> 00:15:57,872
[SPEAKER_01]: The main chief of those characteristics being the danger, the peril, the risk.

200
00:15:57,892 --> 00:16:01,194
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that sign of the danger gets established.

201
00:16:01,294 --> 00:16:05,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind is composed within that knowledge that now you have.

202
00:16:08,170 --> 00:16:09,951
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't forget about it.

203
00:16:11,092 --> 00:16:13,153
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, because everything is dangerous.

204
00:16:13,193 --> 00:16:13,353
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

205
00:16:13,673 --> 00:16:15,774
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't need to keep thinking danger, danger, danger.

206
00:16:16,074 --> 00:16:18,695
[SPEAKER_01]: You just need to keep seeing, hearing, smelling, tasting, touching.

207
00:16:18,755 --> 00:16:20,196
[SPEAKER_01]: Danger becomes implicit.

208
00:16:20,836 --> 00:16:26,078
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, a possibility of pleasure is implicit for the mind not free from sensuality.

209
00:16:26,779 --> 00:16:36,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, mind free from sensuality has replaced the possibility for pleasure with possibility of pain and danger and something you will shy away from.

210
00:16:37,130 --> 00:16:38,811
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's effortless.

211
00:16:38,851 --> 00:16:43,732
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to maintain your freedom from centrality and protect it, because that's not freedom, then.

212
00:16:44,012 --> 00:16:44,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Freedom means you're free.

213
00:16:45,533 --> 00:16:51,355
[SPEAKER_01]: You're literally freed, because you replaced it correctly, you reversed the order, and you fixed it, so to speak.

214
00:16:51,895 --> 00:16:52,575
[SPEAKER_01]: You healed it up.

215
00:16:55,896 --> 00:17:06,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's learning how to... And that's, again, the subtle thing I'm trying to point out here, is that, inevitably, you have to start with, like,

216
00:17:08,470 --> 00:17:11,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Fabricating the perception of danger upon centrality.

217
00:17:15,453 --> 00:17:16,874
[SPEAKER_01]: What's dangerous, is dangerous, is dangerous.

218
00:17:17,374 --> 00:17:19,195
[SPEAKER_01]: And usually in response to centrality.

219
00:17:19,215 --> 00:17:27,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there's... the mind has perceived centrality, craves, it wants it, it wants the pleasure, it's so painful, I must get rid of it, I must get rid of it, I must get rid of it.

220
00:17:31,261 --> 00:17:37,384
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as you have that attitude, you will not be able to see through that which you're trying to get rid of.

221
00:17:38,600 --> 00:17:44,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't want to get rid of the... the sensuality as the cause for your bother.

222
00:17:45,126 --> 00:17:45,266
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

223
00:17:45,326 --> 00:17:50,551
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to get rid of your attraction towards sensuality that's kind of indifferent to your bother.

224
00:17:51,485 --> 00:17:56,729
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, for to target the beautiful things remaining in the world, he still perceived this is beautiful, this is ugly.

225
00:17:57,350 --> 00:17:59,792
[SPEAKER_01]: This is what used to excite me, now it doesn't.

226
00:18:00,592 --> 00:18:08,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't lose perception of what's agreeable, what's disagreeable, but you lose attraction to it, because you've seen the danger through in that attraction to beautiful.

227
00:18:09,139 --> 00:18:12,922
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as you're trying to replace and get rid of the beautiful, you're not seeing the opposite side of it.

228
00:18:13,062 --> 00:18:13,623
[SPEAKER_01]: So, literally,

229
00:18:14,597 --> 00:18:24,063
[SPEAKER_01]: The coin given to you, you're so scared of its face, because you might have a desire for it, so you try to get rid of, replace the coin, but how can you then see what's behind it?

230
00:18:24,123 --> 00:18:25,324
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the other side of that coin?

231
00:18:25,524 --> 00:18:26,765
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to allow it to endure.

232
00:18:27,265 --> 00:18:29,907
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sense restraint is absolutely not negotiable.

233
00:18:30,387 --> 00:18:41,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Because sense restraint is that prerequisite, that sort of precondition of you developing sufficient strength to be able to allow the beautiful thing, if it has a reason, you don't seek it out,

234
00:18:42,427 --> 00:18:46,209
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it has... and it will arise, because you're not free from sensuality.

235
00:18:46,850 --> 00:18:50,612
[SPEAKER_01]: When it arises, you don't want to get rid of it, you want to see through it.

236
00:18:50,792 --> 00:18:56,095
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that, it needs to endure with that like a sharp inciting sort of edge.

237
00:18:57,817 --> 00:19:04,581
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you have developed your behavior beforehand, as the Buddha would say, you will not act out of it, which means you get to see it.

238
00:19:05,296 --> 00:19:08,097
[SPEAKER_01]: And you get to see then your responsibility for your own attraction.

239
00:19:08,438 --> 00:19:12,840
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in things, it's in your own unchecked craving towards it.

240
00:19:16,502 --> 00:19:20,824
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how you get to see the asubha of subha, true subha.

241
00:19:25,626 --> 00:19:33,710
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will not see that on the level of the eye, nose, smell, ears, sound and so on.

242
00:19:37,453 --> 00:19:39,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Those will remain agreeable or disagreeable.

243
00:19:40,394 --> 00:19:44,855
[SPEAKER_01]: But when those are agreeable, within that, you want to know the danger of it, because it is there.

244
00:19:46,595 --> 00:19:50,016
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you transcend both sense organs and sense objects.

245
00:19:51,276 --> 00:19:52,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just the sense objects.

246
00:19:56,038 --> 00:19:57,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Being developed in regard to your own body.

247
00:19:58,995 --> 00:20:00,336
[SPEAKER_01]: As in seeing it for what it is.

248
00:20:00,937 --> 00:20:05,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing the danger for it, not cultivating lust and passion and desire towards it.

249
00:20:05,742 --> 00:20:12,108
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, the simile, like, like a beautiful young woman or a young man in their youth and somebody puts a dead snake around their neck.

250
00:20:12,389 --> 00:20:16,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Wouldn't they be, like, disgusted, appalled, just get rid of it?

251
00:20:16,973 --> 00:20:20,457
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you should cultivate attitude towards your body.

252
00:20:22,341 --> 00:20:33,489
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, oh, within this body, that's healthy, that's okay, that I don't see any problem with it at the moment, you need to, like, oh, I'm a... you can't get rid of it.

253
00:20:33,929 --> 00:20:40,674
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you need to do within that, because you only have one body, so you can't just replace it with a more suitable body for contemplation.

254
00:20:42,074 --> 00:20:46,515
[SPEAKER_01]: And you certainly don't want to wait until you get sick, old and falling apart.

255
00:20:47,035 --> 00:20:55,617
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you then practice this repulsiveness attitude of non-lust and danger within that which you can't see that it's repulsive?

256
00:20:55,637 --> 00:20:57,857
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will never see it as repulsive, because it's you.

257
00:20:59,578 --> 00:21:02,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you cultivate it on the level of that liability that we spoke about.

258
00:21:03,579 --> 00:21:10,020
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, this factually, currently healthy, relatively young, strong and so on body,

259
00:21:11,231 --> 00:21:13,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Within that, I mustn't try to get rid of that, because I can't.

260
00:21:14,355 --> 00:21:19,702
[SPEAKER_01]: So, within that, I need to see the danger of that beautiful, agreeable, healthy life, and so on.

261
00:21:20,732 --> 00:21:21,913
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't deny that.

262
00:21:22,313 --> 00:21:22,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

263
00:21:22,973 --> 00:21:28,736
[SPEAKER_01]: You will fall into the denial if you try to ugly, ugly, and visualize, and just visualize this disgust, disgust.

264
00:21:28,776 --> 00:21:28,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

265
00:21:29,076 --> 00:21:30,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, that's a separate thing.

266
00:21:30,977 --> 00:21:33,778
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not seeing it on the relevant basis of beautiful.

267
00:21:33,799 --> 00:21:35,239
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not seeing it through the beautiful.

268
00:21:35,279 --> 00:21:40,062
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just doing it in response to it, which means you're denying the first thing of beautiful.

269
00:21:40,602 --> 00:21:46,685
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the next... by denying the first things of beautiful that you perceive, you're denying the basis of beautiful.

270
00:21:48,669 --> 00:21:49,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, I'm seeing it.

271
00:21:50,111 --> 00:21:52,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you're maintaining the basis of beautiful for the future.

272
00:21:52,933 --> 00:21:53,254
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even...

273
00:21:53,814 --> 00:21:58,219
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your practice of asubha will be valid for as long as your determination lasts.

274
00:21:59,140 --> 00:22:01,322
[SPEAKER_01]: To look at ugly, look at ugly, look at ugly, look at ugly.

275
00:22:01,582 --> 00:22:02,964
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no wisdom coming out of it.

276
00:22:03,584 --> 00:22:09,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Because wisdom remains even when you stop making the effort.

277
00:22:09,751 --> 00:22:11,693
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stop making the effort, it lasts back.

278
00:22:12,730 --> 00:22:13,751
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, your mind...

279
00:22:14,111 --> 00:22:20,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, which means what the Buddha said, which means that, on account of which, the future lustful manifest hasn't been abandoned in me.

280
00:22:23,915 --> 00:22:31,039
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you abandon it by seeing the danger within the beautiful, not by trying to replace the beautiful.

281
00:22:32,464 --> 00:22:33,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it is there.

282
00:22:33,725 --> 00:22:34,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

283
00:22:34,426 --> 00:22:36,288
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't... you didn't create it.

284
00:22:36,328 --> 00:22:37,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

285
00:22:37,029 --> 00:22:38,671
[SPEAKER_01]: The arising had nothing to do with you.

286
00:22:38,691 --> 00:22:39,152
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

287
00:22:39,692 --> 00:22:49,444
[SPEAKER_01]: You will inevitably have to act out of the attitude of denying it, by blaming it for your own attraction, and trying to get rid of it, and replace it with perceptions of a subha, because that's more wholesome.

288
00:22:49,464 --> 00:22:49,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

289
00:22:51,022 --> 00:22:55,966
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to be restrained by body, by speech, by mind, but then you need to allow it to endure.

290
00:22:56,086 --> 00:22:57,267
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the unpleasant bit.

291
00:22:57,747 --> 00:23:02,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Having sensuality endure, you know, and not act out of it.

292
00:23:04,032 --> 00:23:06,514
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the satipaṭṭhāna, if it's done rightly.

293
00:23:06,854 --> 00:23:11,577
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows the mind of sensuality, mind affected with sensuality, as mind affected with sensuality.

294
00:23:11,597 --> 00:23:15,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Not he knows mind of sensuality and then he gets rid of that sensuality.

295
00:23:15,160 --> 00:23:15,260
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

296
00:23:15,320 --> 00:23:17,162
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows it for what it is.

297
00:23:17,602 --> 00:23:19,103
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is in sensual domain.

298
00:23:19,183 --> 00:23:20,104
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not his doing.

299
00:23:21,098 --> 00:23:22,938
[SPEAKER_01]: But his doing is, if he does what?

300
00:23:23,419 --> 00:23:29,460
[SPEAKER_01]: If he welcomes that domain, if he entertains it, if he delights in it, if he acts out of it.

301
00:23:29,500 --> 00:23:32,081
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I will not get rid of the domain of sensuality.

302
00:23:32,121 --> 00:23:32,561
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

303
00:23:32,921 --> 00:23:38,802
[SPEAKER_01]: But I will get rid of my delight, my attitude towards it, which is why the domain of sensuality keeps coming back.

304
00:23:39,403 --> 00:23:40,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I keep welcoming it in.

305
00:23:42,183 --> 00:23:44,484
[SPEAKER_01]: And same like when the Buddha said, actually,

306
00:23:45,840 --> 00:23:53,285
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, the Mara said to his daughters, who tried to see, like, sort of, entice the mind of the Buddha, he said, oh, you're wasting your time.

307
00:23:53,305 --> 00:23:58,249
[SPEAKER_01]: I spent seven years of getting back to him and trying to get him out and he's nowhere to be seen.

308
00:24:00,310 --> 00:24:01,771
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stop engaging with it.

309
00:24:01,871 --> 00:24:04,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Mara still might be jumping around, you're trying to entertain.

310
00:24:04,953 --> 00:24:08,676
[SPEAKER_01]: It's until, basically, he sees that you're irreversible.

311
00:24:09,016 --> 00:24:14,220
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible for you to step back into that domain and then these things will even stop from appearing to you.

312
00:24:15,273 --> 00:24:19,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, like flies landing when there is still, like, bad smell or open wounds.

313
00:24:20,997 --> 00:24:25,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you might have healed, but the fly is still trying to find a place where they can land.

314
00:24:26,040 --> 00:24:35,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, the simile the Buddha used is like a crow trying to find a... No, sorry, the simile the Mahārāja used was like a crow trying to find an edible bit that he can bite on.

315
00:24:35,666 --> 00:24:40,450
[SPEAKER_01]: And he kept coming back to it, kept searching, kept digging, and in the end, he gives up.

316
00:24:40,690 --> 00:24:42,651
[SPEAKER_01]: After six, seven years, the Mahārāja gave up.

317
00:24:45,797 --> 00:24:53,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, seven years is still... Yeah, but none of those seven years were bothered for the Buddha, because the bother is never in the Mara or Mara's things.

318
00:24:53,780 --> 00:25:08,446
[SPEAKER_01]: The bother is in you, like, inadvertently, through your own choice, lacking awareness, lacking the danger, perception of danger, wandering straight into Mara's domain, on and on and on, and then keeping yourself there.

319
00:25:10,467 --> 00:25:11,167
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the danger.

320
00:25:11,468 --> 00:25:11,988
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the bother.

321
00:25:13,947 --> 00:25:14,988
[SPEAKER_01]: This is just nuisance.

322
00:25:15,509 --> 00:25:24,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Like heat, wind, rain... perceptions of beautiful.

323
00:25:28,826 --> 00:25:31,530
[SPEAKER_01]: So, learning how to allow beautiful to endure without acting out of it,

324
00:25:33,833 --> 00:25:35,213
[SPEAKER_01]: It can then reveal the other side.

325
00:25:35,414 --> 00:25:51,759
[SPEAKER_01]: Or then, rather, if you learn how to allow beautiful to endure when it has arisen, not act out of it, know the mind affected with sensuality as mind affected with sensuality, then thoughts of a subha will actually start to apply correctly, as the counterpart of this very same thing here.

326
00:25:53,260 --> 00:25:56,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Counterpart that doesn't deny the beautiful, just reveals its other side.

327
00:26:02,196 --> 00:26:06,097
[SPEAKER_00]: So, continuing in regards to those perceptions that need to be developed.

328
00:26:08,018 --> 00:26:14,320
[SPEAKER_00]: When a bhikkhu becomes accustomed to the perception of death, their mind draws back from attachment to life.

329
00:26:17,021 --> 00:26:23,003
[SPEAKER_00]: And either equanimity gets established or that other side gets established.

330
00:26:24,724 --> 00:26:28,345
[SPEAKER_00]: So, either he becomes quite dispassionate or, again,

331
00:26:29,632 --> 00:26:33,576
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly the same what we discussed in the previous section.

332
00:26:35,158 --> 00:26:51,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, you can afford to practice a subha at the expense of denying subha, but, see, you can't practice mindfulness of death at the expense of denying life, because you have to be alive in order to practice mindfulness of death.

333
00:26:53,432 --> 00:26:58,397
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the perception of that death is counterpart of this life here.

334
00:26:58,417 --> 00:27:01,039
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can just get rid of it in order to see what death is.

335
00:27:02,045 --> 00:27:04,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is how people practice asubha, usually.

336
00:27:04,687 --> 00:27:08,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Get rid of the sensuality and then just hammer in asubha, asubha, asubha.

337
00:27:08,711 --> 00:27:09,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, look at something happening.

338
00:27:09,752 --> 00:27:10,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Look at something else.

339
00:27:10,573 --> 00:27:14,797
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now, you must... to practice the perception of death, you must die.

340
00:27:14,817 --> 00:27:15,938
[SPEAKER_00]: You must not be alive.

341
00:27:15,998 --> 00:27:16,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

342
00:27:16,538 --> 00:27:18,760
[SPEAKER_00]: You must die and then look at it, stare at it for long enough.

343
00:27:18,780 --> 00:27:20,882
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the other... that's why many people say, oh, the other side.

344
00:27:20,902 --> 00:27:25,747
[SPEAKER_01]: It means the other side is there, simultaneously present, at the same time, within this.

345
00:27:25,847 --> 00:27:26,007
[SPEAKER_01]: Like,

346
00:27:27,040 --> 00:27:28,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Within whatever you're attending.

347
00:27:28,921 --> 00:27:33,464
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the death is simultaneously present as the other side of life.

348
00:27:33,484 --> 00:27:34,745
[SPEAKER_01]: That you are alive right here, right now.

349
00:27:35,105 --> 00:27:36,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't attend it, necessarily.

350
00:27:37,386 --> 00:27:39,888
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not attending the fact that you're alive, you're just taking it for granted.

351
00:27:40,428 --> 00:27:41,289
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't matter.

352
00:27:41,489 --> 00:27:42,990
[SPEAKER_01]: In its nature, death is implicit.

353
00:27:44,010 --> 00:27:45,772
[SPEAKER_01]: And it wouldn't be implicit if there is no life.

354
00:27:46,452 --> 00:27:49,474
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, a subha would not be implicit if there is no subha.

355
00:27:54,777 --> 00:28:17,597
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you actually, instead of following the direction that taking your life for granted offers, you start discerning, whenever there is life, death is implicit, and start developing that subtle, implicit, opposite perception of death within the life, you will experience disenchantment towards life.

356
00:28:19,178 --> 00:28:22,241
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be impossible for you to remain attracted to life.

357
00:28:24,888 --> 00:28:26,589
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to be able to see that life.

358
00:28:27,429 --> 00:28:28,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

359
00:28:28,509 --> 00:28:30,050
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to be able to see for what it is.

360
00:28:30,230 --> 00:28:33,250
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you have to be able to see subha for what it is.

361
00:28:33,470 --> 00:28:37,432
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't practice asubha until you've actually understood what subha is.

362
00:28:38,312 --> 00:28:42,633
[SPEAKER_01]: But you will not understand what subha is, because you just keep trying to get rid of it, because it's too painful.

363
00:28:43,513 --> 00:28:49,555
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that subha, sorry, is that attraction, in a way.

364
00:28:50,937 --> 00:28:55,442
[SPEAKER_01]: It's so beautiful, but it's that... For you, there is no attraction without subha, yeah.

365
00:28:55,542 --> 00:29:05,413
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can even be attracted to genuine as subha, because you're seeing it as subha, because of your attraction.

366
00:29:05,733 --> 00:29:06,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

367
00:29:06,294 --> 00:29:13,101
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you're free from attraction, subha will still remain, but you wouldn't be attracted to it.

368
00:29:15,698 --> 00:29:23,221
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to be able to see the phenomenon of life, to discern the implicit danger of it.

369
00:29:23,281 --> 00:29:24,382
[SPEAKER_01]: To see the other side of it.

370
00:29:25,862 --> 00:29:27,623
[SPEAKER_00]: The dangerous...

371
00:29:27,903 --> 00:29:29,024
[SPEAKER_01]: The other side of the coin.

372
00:29:30,344 --> 00:29:31,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Not a different coin.

373
00:29:31,205 --> 00:29:32,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Not a second coin.

374
00:29:32,205 --> 00:29:32,826
[SPEAKER_01]: The same coin.

375
00:29:36,787 --> 00:29:38,288
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah, you get to see the dangerous.

376
00:29:38,328 --> 00:29:40,069
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is dangerous.

377
00:29:40,109 --> 00:29:42,830
[SPEAKER_01]: If it weren't dangerous, the Buddha wouldn't have said you should abandon it.

378
00:29:45,503 --> 00:29:49,604
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not seeing the danger, it's because you are not seeing the danger, not because it's not dangerous.

379
00:29:50,585 --> 00:29:52,745
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's something you need to take on faith in the beginning.

380
00:29:53,405 --> 00:29:53,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

381
00:29:53,866 --> 00:29:55,386
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha says that it is dangerous.

382
00:29:56,446 --> 00:30:00,088
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have nothing to lose if you start practicing towards seeing the danger.

383
00:30:00,588 --> 00:30:01,328
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only gain.

384
00:30:04,509 --> 00:30:07,550
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, whatever... you see it, life, correctly...

385
00:30:10,676 --> 00:30:15,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever's happening in life, you're not... you won't be taken... you're impassioned by it.

386
00:30:16,158 --> 00:30:18,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because the passion is... passion is not the product of life.

387
00:30:20,579 --> 00:30:32,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Passion is product of you thinking or having a view that that life is yours, that that life is in your control, that that life is not subject to change, that that life will not end, that that life will last forever.

388
00:30:33,502 --> 00:30:36,443
[SPEAKER_01]: All these are implicit, basically, notions that you have towards life.

389
00:30:38,630 --> 00:30:39,510
[SPEAKER_01]: Enhanced passion.

390
00:30:39,610 --> 00:30:43,812
[SPEAKER_01]: So, passion is because of your notions, misconceptions about life.

391
00:30:44,032 --> 00:30:45,373
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you have passion towards life.

392
00:30:46,073 --> 00:30:49,714
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove the misconceptions of life, no base for passion.

393
00:30:51,255 --> 00:30:57,277
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, as I said, you cannot remove the misconceptions of life by denying life.

394
00:30:59,158 --> 00:31:05,560
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot remove misconceptions about beautiful by trying to constantly get rid of it and denying it, because it bothers you.

395
00:31:06,696 --> 00:31:13,261
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove misconceivings by allowing beautiful to endure and then working on your misconceivings of it, in regard to it.

396
00:31:13,281 --> 00:31:14,682
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... we said it many times.

397
00:31:14,702 --> 00:31:20,626
[SPEAKER_01]: We said, like, if the central thought manifests, he... and that's what people misunderstand.

398
00:31:20,766 --> 00:31:22,428
[SPEAKER_01]: He does... he does away... he gets rid of it.

399
00:31:23,769 --> 00:31:27,251
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, oh, but how does he go about getting rid of it?

400
00:31:27,752 --> 00:31:28,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Does Buddha say he denies it?

401
00:31:28,932 --> 00:31:29,093
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

402
00:31:29,673 --> 00:31:35,037
[SPEAKER_01]: He gets rid of it by not welcoming it, by not delighting in it, by not entertaining it.

403
00:31:35,732 --> 00:31:36,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, that's how you get rid of it.

404
00:31:37,373 --> 00:31:38,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Indirectly, so to speak.

405
00:31:38,473 --> 00:31:40,714
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't get rid of it, but I will not think this, I will not think this.

406
00:31:41,214 --> 00:31:42,515
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not think this.

407
00:31:43,856 --> 00:31:45,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why you're thinking it.

408
00:31:47,437 --> 00:31:52,759
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why that kind of denial always results in build-up of frustrations, because you're contradicting yourself.

409
00:31:53,380 --> 00:31:54,700
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not think sensual thoughts.

410
00:31:55,641 --> 00:31:56,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Which thoughts?

411
00:31:56,081 --> 00:31:58,642
[SPEAKER_01]: The sensual thoughts that you're thinking, because you don't want to think them.

412
00:32:00,803 --> 00:32:03,384
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you get rid of it, but how?

413
00:32:04,462 --> 00:32:13,126
[SPEAKER_01]: You get rid of the sensual thoughts, or you get rid of the intoxication with life, but not carelessly welcoming it, delighting in it, entertaining it.

414
00:32:13,326 --> 00:32:14,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, so it arises on its own.

415
00:32:15,447 --> 00:32:16,347
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not welcome it.

416
00:32:17,288 --> 00:32:20,249
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean I'll go out and chase it away, or try to kill it, or get... No.

417
00:32:20,729 --> 00:32:27,012
[SPEAKER_01]: I will just make sure I remain mindful of not welcoming it with any gesture, or any implication, or any hint.

418
00:32:27,712 --> 00:32:29,393
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not give in to welcoming this.

419
00:32:30,173 --> 00:32:32,414
[SPEAKER_01]: And you do that long enough, the thing will go away.

420
00:32:33,055 --> 00:32:34,035
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you got rid of it.

421
00:32:35,374 --> 00:32:40,797
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, you don't have to go and seek out, you know, death or possibility of death.

422
00:32:40,817 --> 00:32:41,897
[SPEAKER_00]: No, because it's already there.

423
00:32:42,177 --> 00:32:42,778
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point.

424
00:32:42,818 --> 00:32:52,382
[SPEAKER_01]: If you seek out, means you still imply, with your view, that the problem is in those individual things that you are now seeking out to prove to yourself that you are not subjected to them.

425
00:32:52,462 --> 00:32:53,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you obviously are.

426
00:32:53,583 --> 00:32:54,824
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

427
00:32:54,844 --> 00:32:55,584
[SPEAKER_00]: You're always close to death.

428
00:32:55,604 --> 00:32:57,445
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's straying from those things you ask yourself.

429
00:32:58,390 --> 00:32:59,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I subject to this?

430
00:32:59,571 --> 00:33:00,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

431
00:33:00,112 --> 00:33:01,212
[SPEAKER_01]: It means it's already there.

432
00:33:01,333 --> 00:33:04,015
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is there to seek out when it's already there?

433
00:33:04,655 --> 00:33:08,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Can future lust manifest in me, in the future?

434
00:33:08,939 --> 00:33:09,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I'm not sure.

435
00:33:09,579 --> 00:33:10,160
[SPEAKER_01]: It means yes.

436
00:33:11,061 --> 00:33:14,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you know that it can't, that's the basis for yes, it can.

437
00:33:15,965 --> 00:33:19,328
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already there, right there, experienced right there and then, here and now.

438
00:33:19,768 --> 00:33:20,769
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is there to seek?

439
00:33:21,169 --> 00:33:22,790
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you seek out the individual,

440
00:33:23,598 --> 00:33:34,006
[SPEAKER_01]: Examples of lust, or so on, to prove to yourself that you're not lustful, means you're fully lustful, and you still think the problem is in those individual things that you perceive, not in your mind of welcoming, delighting, entertaining it.

441
00:33:34,026 --> 00:33:36,207
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can ask yourself, am I subject to death?

442
00:33:37,248 --> 00:33:37,408
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

443
00:33:37,808 --> 00:33:40,510
[SPEAKER_01]: Will I be affected if my life were to end next second?

444
00:33:41,451 --> 00:33:41,631
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

445
00:33:41,831 --> 00:33:42,272
[SPEAKER_01]: There you go.

446
00:33:42,552 --> 00:33:42,932
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

447
00:33:43,132 --> 00:33:43,573
[SPEAKER_01]: There you go.

448
00:33:44,073 --> 00:33:44,413
[SPEAKER_01]: You will.

449
00:33:47,435 --> 00:33:50,958
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you then free yourself from death without denying life?

450
00:33:52,513 --> 00:33:57,277
[SPEAKER_01]: By seeing the danger in life and removing slightest passion towards life, while the life is still there.

451
00:33:58,498 --> 00:34:02,440
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you know, ah, it's the passion that kept me bound to life that's subject to death.

452
00:34:03,441 --> 00:34:04,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Not life in itself.

453
00:34:04,842 --> 00:34:07,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, freedom from life is possible, without needing to die.

454
00:34:09,266 --> 00:34:13,489
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, when you do die, as the Buddha would say, you will not be reappearing anywhere else.

455
00:34:16,791 --> 00:34:20,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you have severed any bond with the entire domain of society.

456
00:34:20,754 --> 00:34:21,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Any inclination towards...

457
00:34:26,978 --> 00:34:29,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Death is where life is, of any kind.

458
00:34:30,800 --> 00:34:40,683
[SPEAKER_01]: If you remove slightest passion towards that life, there is no basis for death to apply to you.

459
00:34:40,723 --> 00:34:48,886
[SPEAKER_00]: There's another perception, the perception of the other side of eating.

460
00:34:50,807 --> 00:34:52,768
[SPEAKER_00]: When you develop that perception,

461
00:34:54,312 --> 00:35:01,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind draws back from the craving for tastes and or you just get equanimous.

462
00:35:03,575 --> 00:35:04,335
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the same thing.

463
00:35:07,156 --> 00:35:13,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Developing the other side of eating or the other side of food, consuming food.

464
00:35:16,400 --> 00:35:19,941
[SPEAKER_00]: What else is there while you're eating, experiencing all these tastes and so on?

465
00:35:20,741 --> 00:35:23,282
[SPEAKER_00]: What are all the reasons that you're eating in the first place?

466
00:35:25,368 --> 00:35:28,610
[SPEAKER_00]: All the digestion, going down, chewing, digesting... Yeah.

467
00:35:28,830 --> 00:35:35,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, all of that is... is sort of... is secondary.

468
00:35:36,855 --> 00:35:39,837
[SPEAKER_01]: To the point that you prioritize the pleasure of eating.

469
00:35:41,017 --> 00:35:42,038
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you put first.

470
00:35:43,579 --> 00:35:51,984
[SPEAKER_01]: See, when you're starving, really, really hungry, losing the sight of that as the reason for your eating,

471
00:35:53,135 --> 00:35:57,558
[SPEAKER_00]: You will be automatically eating out of pleasure for the taste, not of the need for food.

472
00:36:01,221 --> 00:36:07,586
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the other side of the eating is, doesn't matter how much you enjoy it or not enjoy it, is something you have to do.

473
00:36:10,388 --> 00:36:11,449
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a need.

474
00:36:14,571 --> 00:36:15,332
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a necessity.

475
00:36:16,953 --> 00:36:20,736
[SPEAKER_01]: But forgetting that it's a necessity, or rather,

476
00:36:21,970 --> 00:36:27,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Taking for granted that necessity, covering it up as a necessity, you then get picky.

477
00:36:27,892 --> 00:36:30,653
[SPEAKER_01]: You then start speaking tastes and start acting out of taste.

478
00:36:31,373 --> 00:36:36,695
[SPEAKER_01]: But doesn't matter how much acting out of taste you go for, it's still within the necessity.

479
00:36:37,215 --> 00:36:38,035
[SPEAKER_01]: You still need to eat.

480
00:36:38,394 --> 00:36:39,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's the horrible thing.

481
00:36:39,735 --> 00:36:41,475
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the horrible thing, exactly.

482
00:36:41,495 --> 00:36:41,815
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to eat.

483
00:36:41,835 --> 00:36:42,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the fact that I have to eat.

484
00:36:42,636 --> 00:36:45,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, your hunger is affecting you, your body, physically.

485
00:36:45,337 --> 00:36:46,717
[SPEAKER_00]: And if I don't, I'm... that's it.

486
00:36:46,737 --> 00:36:47,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, destroying you, yeah.

487
00:36:48,738 --> 00:36:58,281
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the pressure of eating is there, but you ignore that, because you took food for granted, and then, on account of it, you develop now priority of tastes.

488
00:37:00,022 --> 00:37:00,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

489
00:37:00,302 --> 00:37:02,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Priority of pleasure, which is fully within that need.

490
00:37:03,283 --> 00:37:07,945
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all you need to do is uncover the need that you covered up as the first.

491
00:37:08,583 --> 00:37:11,446
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, taste is the second, not the other way around.

492
00:37:12,828 --> 00:37:25,082
[SPEAKER_01]: But by covering up that need, the pressure, the hunger, the unpleasantness of it, the being subjected to it, covering that up, you put joy for the taste first, you put pleasure first.

493
00:37:26,095 --> 00:37:29,836
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a slave taking delight in the master, somehow.

494
00:37:30,377 --> 00:37:30,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

495
00:37:30,697 --> 00:37:31,957
[SPEAKER_00]: A slave forgetting is a slave.

496
00:37:32,117 --> 00:37:32,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

497
00:37:32,878 --> 00:37:33,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Forgetting is a slave.

498
00:37:33,818 --> 00:37:37,179
[SPEAKER_00]: And then taking delight in the fact that he's being beaten every day.

499
00:37:37,239 --> 00:37:37,619
[SPEAKER_00]: It's lovely.

500
00:37:37,659 --> 00:37:38,820
[SPEAKER_00]: He has to do all this work.

501
00:37:39,560 --> 00:37:39,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

502
00:37:39,780 --> 00:37:48,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, more like finding a preference in how he's going to be beaten that day.

503
00:37:49,064 --> 00:37:50,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Finding joy in that.

504
00:37:50,544 --> 00:37:52,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Forgetting that he has no choice about being beaten.

505
00:37:54,863 --> 00:37:56,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Being subjected to discomfort.

506
00:37:58,567 --> 00:38:00,349
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, you're subjected to discomfort.

507
00:38:02,471 --> 00:38:07,956
[SPEAKER_01]: If you go through beating, beating stops for a bit, then it comes back.

508
00:38:09,537 --> 00:38:19,987
[SPEAKER_01]: But, instead of seeing, is there a way out of this entire situation of being subjected to beating in the first place, he just focuses on what would be the agreeable ways of receiving my beating.

509
00:38:22,465 --> 00:38:23,906
[SPEAKER_00]: That's that craving for taste.

510
00:38:24,026 --> 00:38:24,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

511
00:38:25,387 --> 00:38:45,137
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of investing his effort in finding the way out of his slavery altogether, where he won't be receiving any beatings, any punishment, his efforts are invested in pursuing, you know, maybe a silky whip or something else that's not as as coarse as other forms of beating.

512
00:38:45,277 --> 00:38:46,598
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's still something you have to go through.

513
00:38:46,618 --> 00:38:49,139
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, oh, so through your own chasing of

514
00:38:50,552 --> 00:38:58,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Refined forms of punishment, you keep yourself still bound to the punishment.

515
00:38:58,021 --> 00:39:02,786
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that food... developing that... necessity of food...

516
00:39:02,826 --> 00:39:03,407
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

517
00:39:03,427 --> 00:39:08,813
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't ignore the fact that food is a necessity, that you are subjected to it unpleasantly,

518
00:39:11,088 --> 00:39:15,031
[SPEAKER_01]: You will then not lose the context of why you eat, why you need to eat.

519
00:39:15,251 --> 00:39:24,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't matter how agreeable or disagreeable the punishment is, how much enjoyable this is, it's still within the unpleasant, not enjoying fact of hunger.

520
00:39:25,597 --> 00:39:27,438
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the hunger disappears, you stop eating.

521
00:39:28,959 --> 00:39:29,320
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

522
00:39:29,460 --> 00:39:31,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you see the disease of that hunger.

523
00:39:32,061 --> 00:39:34,043
[SPEAKER_00]: The greatest disease is hunger, wasn't it?

524
00:39:34,623 --> 00:39:35,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, something like that.

525
00:39:36,824 --> 00:39:37,625
[SPEAKER_01]: It is affliction.

526
00:39:38,883 --> 00:39:40,364
[SPEAKER_01]: You're constantly afflicted by hunger.

527
00:39:40,464 --> 00:39:40,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

528
00:39:40,725 --> 00:39:41,845
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the greatest affliction.

529
00:39:42,266 --> 00:39:42,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

530
00:39:42,766 --> 00:39:48,691
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're just... your way of dealing with affliction is not freeing yourself from being liable to affliction altogether.

531
00:39:50,152 --> 00:39:53,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Your way is just finding the pleasurable aspects of affliction.

532
00:39:54,055 --> 00:39:54,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

533
00:39:54,235 --> 00:39:55,256
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still affliction.

534
00:39:57,077 --> 00:40:00,800
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not really dealing with anything, just covering it up.

535
00:40:02,045 --> 00:40:08,292
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that enjoyment of taste and cooking and recipes and so on... Yeah, that comes out of putting that first, putting that, that...

536
00:40:08,312 --> 00:40:11,176
[SPEAKER_00]: But when you see that other side, that necessity of food, it becomes quite ridiculous.

537
00:40:11,196 --> 00:40:12,738
[SPEAKER_00]: The passion for this taste will disappear, yeah, yeah.

538
00:40:12,758 --> 00:40:13,539
[SPEAKER_00]: It seems like quite a bit...

539
00:40:13,619 --> 00:40:19,205
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be like, like... Well, it would be impossible to make the effort towards it, towards the taste.

540
00:40:19,986 --> 00:40:23,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean that, like, you will not taste it, if something is agreeable.

541
00:40:23,507 --> 00:40:35,370
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, it would be impossible for any amount of agreeable or disagreeable taste to disturb your mind, or to, like, tip back that picture into putting first, which is second, and putting second, which is first.

542
00:40:36,810 --> 00:40:37,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Taste is always second.

543
00:40:38,070 --> 00:40:40,791
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure to taste or displeasure of taste is always second.

544
00:40:41,211 --> 00:40:41,711
[SPEAKER_01]: To hunger.

545
00:40:43,231 --> 00:40:45,172
[SPEAKER_01]: But for you, taste is the first.

546
00:40:45,252 --> 00:40:46,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is the first.

547
00:40:46,412 --> 00:40:47,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else is second.

548
00:40:47,172 --> 00:40:48,093
[SPEAKER_01]: You even forget about hunger.

549
00:40:48,113 --> 00:40:49,433
[SPEAKER_01]: You even forget to cheat out of hunger.

550
00:40:51,087 --> 00:40:53,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why then people eat even when they're not hungry.

551
00:40:53,608 --> 00:40:54,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Just seeking out the taste.

552
00:40:54,748 --> 00:40:56,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the taste is now the criteria.

553
00:40:56,628 --> 00:40:58,249
[SPEAKER_01]: The pleasure of the taste is a criteria.

554
00:40:59,789 --> 00:41:03,450
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't matter how great, how refined that criteria you might have developed.

555
00:41:04,250 --> 00:41:05,471
